# Hyperbolic geometry from its axioms, by its standard MODEL. The plane is the upper sheet of the hyperboloid
# B(x, x) = 1 in Minkowski space R^(2,1), with the Lorentz form B(x, y) = x0 y0 - x1 y1 - x2 y2:
#
#   a POINT is a vector with B(x, x) = 1 and x0 > 0,
#   a LINE is the plane through the origin orthogonal to a vector n with B(n, n) = -1, its points the x with B(x, n) = 0,
#   the DISTANCE between points is d with cosh d = B(x, y),
#   an ISOMETRY is a linear map keeping B (the Lorentz group), generated by reflections in lines.
#
# A model is how an axiom system is shown consistent and how its theorems are computed, so here the axioms are
# THEOREMS of the model, and everything is a polynomial identity in the coordinates, proved for all real values (a
# polynomial identity over the integers holds in every commutative ring). The one tool is the Lorentz cross product
# x * y = (J x) x (J y), J = diag(1, -1, -1), which is orthogonal to x and y for B, and whose norm is
#
#   B(x * y, x * y) = B(x, x) B(y, y) - B(x, y)^2.
#
# That single identity carries the incidence geometry:
#
#   INCIDENCE    two distinct points x, y lie on exactly one line, with normal x * y (spacelike, norm -sinh^2 d);
#   PARALLELS    two lines n, m meet where p = n * m points; B(p, p) = 1 - B(n, m)^2, so they MEET when |B(n, m)| < 1,
#                are LIMITING PARALLELS when |B(n, m)| = 1 (meeting at infinity), and are ULTRAPARALLEL when
#                |B(n, m)| > 1, with p then the normal of their unique common perpendicular;
#   THE HYPERBOLIC PARALLEL AXIOM, witnessed with integers: a point off a line through which pass two distinct lines
#                that do not meet it, one limiting and one ultraparallel. Euclid's fifth fails;
#   ISOMETRIES   rotations, boosts and the reflection in any line keep B; a reflection is an involution fixing its line;
#   TRIGONOMETRY the tangent vectors at a vertex give the hyperbolic law of cosines,
#                cosh c = cosh a cosh b - sinh a sinh b cos C;
#   4-SPACE      the same lift, distance and reflections for hyperbolic 4-space, the space of the mesh {3,4,3,4};
#   THE MODELS   the Poincare disk, the Klein disk and the upper half plane are maps of the same plane: the disk lift
#                lands on the hyperboloid, its distance is cosh d = 1 + 2 |p - q|^2 / ((1 - |p|^2)(1 - |q|^2)), so
#                d >= 0 with equality only at p = q; the Klein point lies inside the unit disk; a line is a circle
#                orthogonal to the boundary; and a Mobius map of the upper half plane keeps the upper half plane and
#                scales differences so that its distance is invariant.
#
# What a polynomial identity cannot reach is stated, not claimed: the order and continuity axioms (betweenness,
# Dedekind completeness) need the order of the reals, and the area of a triangle (pi minus its angle sum, proved for
# tilings in space/hyperbolic/gauss-bonnet) needs integration.

# The Lorentz form B(x, y) = x0 y0 - x1 y1 - x2 y2.
task lorentz-form
  take x0, like integer
  take x1, like integer
  take x2, like integer
  take y0, like integer
  take y1, like integer
  take y2, like integer
  like integer
  send back
    call subtract
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read x0
          read y0
        call multiply
          read x1
          read y1
      call multiply
        read x2
        read y2

# The t coordinate of the Lorentz cross product x * y = (J x) x (J y).
task lorentz-cross-t
  take x0, like integer
  take x1, like integer
  take x2, like integer
  take y0, like integer
  take y1, like integer
  take y2, like integer
  like integer
  send back
    call subtract
      call multiply
        read x1
        read y2
      call multiply
        read x2
        read y1

# The x coordinate of the Lorentz cross product x * y = (J x) x (J y).
task lorentz-cross-x
  take x0, like integer
  take x1, like integer
  take x2, like integer
  take y0, like integer
  take y1, like integer
  take y2, like integer
  like integer
  send back
    call subtract
      call multiply
        read x0
        read y2
      call multiply
        read x2
        read y0

# The y coordinate of the Lorentz cross product x * y = (J x) x (J y).
task lorentz-cross-y
  take x0, like integer
  take x1, like integer
  take x2, like integer
  take y0, like integer
  take y1, like integer
  take y2, like integer
  like integer
  send back
    call subtract
      call multiply
        read x1
        read y0
      call multiply
        read x0
        read y1

# THE CROSS PRODUCT IS ORTHOGONAL TO BOTH FACTORS, so x * y is the normal of a plane containing x and y.
rule the-cross-product-is-orthogonal-to-its-first-factor
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        read x0
        read x1
        read x2
      code 0

rule the-cross-product-is-orthogonal-to-its-second-factor
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        read y0
        read y1
        read y2
      code 0

# THE NORM OF THE CROSS PRODUCT: B(x * y, x * y) = B(x, x) B(y, y) - B(x, y)^2, for all x, y.
rule the-norm-of-a-cross-product
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
          call lorentz-form
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2
          call lorentz-form
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2

# THE LINE THROUGH TWO POINTS IS UNIQUE. The double cross product expands as (x * y) * n = B(x, n) y - B(y, n) x
# (coordinate by coordinate below), so a normal n orthogonal to both x and y has (x * y) * n = 0: n is parallel to
# x * y, and the plane through x and y is the only one.

rule the-double-cross-product-expands-t
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  seat n0, like integer
  seat n1, like integer
  seat n2, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-cross-t
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        read n0
        read n1
        read n2
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
            read n0
            read n1
            read n2
          read y0
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2
            read n0
            read n1
            read n2
          read x0

rule the-double-cross-product-expands-x
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  seat n0, like integer
  seat n1, like integer
  seat n2, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-cross-x
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        read n0
        read n1
        read n2
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
            read n0
            read n1
            read n2
          read y1
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2
            read n0
            read n1
            read n2
          read x1

rule the-double-cross-product-expands-y
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  seat n0, like integer
  seat n1, like integer
  seat n2, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-cross-y
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        read n0
        read n1
        read n2
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
            read n0
            read n1
            read n2
          read y2
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2
            read n0
            read n1
            read n2
          read x2

# TWO POINTS SPAN A LINE: for points (B = 1), the normal x * y has norm 1 - cosh^2 d = -sinh^2 d, which is
# negative (a genuine line) exactly when the points are distinct.
rule two-points-span-a-line-of-norm-minus-sinh-squared
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  have x-point
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        read x0
        read x1
        read x2
        read x0
        read x1
        read x2
      code 1
  have y-point
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        read y0
        read y1
        read y2
        read y0
        read y1
        read y2
      code 1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read x0
          read x1
          read x2
          read y0
          read y1
          read y2
      call subtract
        code 1
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2
          call lorentz-form
            read x0
            read x1
            read x2
            read y0
            read y1
            read y2

# TWO LINES AND THEIR MEETING: for unit normals n, m (B = -1), p = n * m lies on both and B(p, p) = 1 - B(n, m)^2.
# So the lines MEET in a point when B(n, m)^2 < 1, are LIMITING PARALLEL when it is 1 (p on the light cone, a point at
# infinity), and are ULTRAPARALLEL when it exceeds 1 (p spacelike, the normal of their common perpendicular).
rule two-lines-meet-where-their-cross-product-points
  seat n0, like integer
  seat n1, like integer
  seat n2, like integer
  seat m0, like integer
  seat m1, like integer
  seat m2, like integer
  have n-line
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        read n0
        read n1
        read n2
        read n0
        read n1
        read n2
      code -1
  have m-line
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        read m0
        read m1
        read m2
        read m0
        read m1
        read m2
      code -1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
      call subtract
        code 1
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            read n0
            read n1
            read n2
            read m0
            read m1
            read m2
          call lorentz-form
            read n0
            read n1
            read n2
            read m0
            read m1
            read m2

rule the-meeting-point-lies-on-the-first-line
  seat n0, like integer
  seat n1, like integer
  seat n2, like integer
  seat m0, like integer
  seat m1, like integer
  seat m2, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        read n0
        read n1
        read n2
      code 0

rule the-meeting-point-lies-on-the-second-line
  seat n0, like integer
  seat n1, like integer
  seat n2, like integer
  seat m0, like integer
  seat m1, like integer
  seat m2, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          read n0
          read n1
          read n2
          read m0
          read m1
          read m2
        read m0
        read m1
        read m2
      code 0

# THE HYPERBOLIC PARALLEL AXIOM, witnessed. The point o = (1, 0, 0) and the line with normal n = (1, 1, 1)
# (B(n, n) = -1). o is not on it (B(o, n) = 1). The lines through o with normals m1 = (0, 1, 0) and m2 = (0, 3, 4)
# both contain o, are distinct, and neither meets n: B(m1, n)^2 = 1 = B(m1, m1) B(n, n), a limiting parallel, and
# B(m2, n)^2 = 49 > 25 = B(m2, m2) B(n, n), an ultraparallel (the meeting criterion scaled to unnormalized m2).
# Through a point off a line pass at least two lines that do not meet it: Euclid's fifth postulate fails.

rule the-witness-point-is-a-point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        code 1
        code 0
        code 0
        code 1
        code 0
        code 0
      code 1

rule the-witness-line-is-a-line
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        code 1
        code 1
        code 1
        code 1
        code 1
        code 1
      code -1

rule the-witness-point-is-off-the-line
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        code 1
        code 0
        code 0
        code 1
        code 1
        code 1
      code 1

rule the-first-parallel-passes-through-the-point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        code 0
        code 1
        code 0
        code 1
        code 0
        code 0
      code 0

rule the-second-parallel-passes-through-the-point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        code 0
        code 3
        code 4
        code 1
        code 0
        code 0
      code 0

rule the-first-parallel-is-limiting
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            code 0
            code 1
            code 0
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
          call lorentz-form
            code 0
            code 1
            code 0
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            code 0
            code 1
            code 0
            code 0
            code 1
            code 0
          call lorentz-form
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
      code 0

rule the-second-parallel-is-ultraparallel
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            code 0
            code 3
            code 4
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
          call lorentz-form
            code 0
            code 3
            code 4
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
        call multiply
          call lorentz-form
            code 0
            code 3
            code 4
            code 0
            code 3
            code 4
          call lorentz-form
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
            code 1
      code 24

# The two lines through o are distinct: their normals are independent, since their cross product is not zero.
# It is 4 o, norm 16: two lines through o meet exactly at o.
rule the-two-parallels-are-distinct-lines
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          code 0
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 4
        call lorentz-cross-x
          code 0
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 4
        call lorentz-cross-y
          code 0
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 4
        call lorentz-cross-t
          code 0
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 4
        call lorentz-cross-x
          code 0
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 4
        call lorentz-cross-y
          code 0
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 4
      code 16

# THREE POINTS NOT ON ONE LINE: (1, 0, 0), (3, 2, 2) and (3, 2, -2) are points (norm 1), and the third is not on the
# line through the first two (it is off their normal).

rule the-second-sample-is-a-point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        code 3
        code 2
        code 2
        code 3
        code 2
        code 2
      code 1

rule the-third-sample-is-a-point
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        code 3
        code 2
        code -2
        code 3
        code 2
        code -2
      code 1

rule the-third-sample-is-off-the-line-of-the-first-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call lorentz-cross-t
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 2
          code 2
        call lorentz-cross-x
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 2
          code 2
        call lorentz-cross-y
          code 1
          code 0
          code 0
          code 3
          code 2
          code 2
        code 3
        code 2
        code -2
      code -8

# ISOMETRIES. A rotation about o by (c, s), c^2 + s^2 = 1, keeps B.
rule a-rotation-keeps-the-lorentz-form
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat s, like integer
  have unit
    call is-equal
      call add
        call multiply
          read c
          read c
        call multiply
          read s
          read s
      code 1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        read x0
        call subtract
          call multiply
            read c
            read x1
          call multiply
            read s
            read x2
        call add
          call multiply
            read s
            read x1
          call multiply
            read c
            read x2
        read y0
        call subtract
          call multiply
            read c
            read y1
          call multiply
            read s
            read y2
        call add
          call multiply
            read s
            read y1
          call multiply
            read c
            read y2
      call lorentz-form
        read x0
        read x1
        read x2
        read y0
        read y1
        read y2

# A boost by (a, b), a^2 - b^2 = 1 (a = cosh, b = sinh of the translation length), keeps B.
rule a-boost-keeps-the-lorentz-form
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  have unit
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          read a
          read a
        call multiply
          read b
          read b
      code 1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            read x0
          call multiply
            read b
            read x1
        call add
          call multiply
            read b
            read x0
          call multiply
            read a
            read x1
        read x2
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            read y0
          call multiply
            read b
            read y1
        call add
          call multiply
            read b
            read y0
          call multiply
            read a
            read y1
        read y2
      call lorentz-form
        read x0
        read x1
        read x2
        read y0
        read y1
        read y2

# THE REFLECTION IN A LINE n (B(n, n) = -1): x -> x + 2 B(x, n) n. It keeps B, it is an involution, and it fixes
# every point of the line. Reflections generate the isometry group (Cartan-Dieudonne), as mirrors generate the tilings.
rule a-reflection-keeps-the-lorentz-form
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat y0, like integer
  seat y1, like integer
  seat y2, like integer
  seat n0, like integer
  seat n1, like integer
  seat n2, like integer
  have line
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        read n0
        read n1
        read n2
        read n0
        read n1
        read n2
      code -1
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call lorentz-form
        call add
          read x0
          call multiply
            call multiply
              code 2
              call lorentz-form
                read x0
                read x1
                read x2
                read n0
                read n1
                read n2
            read n0
        call add
          read x1
          call multiply
            call multiply
              code 2
              call lorentz-form
                read x0
                read x1
                read x2
                read n0
                read n1
                read n2
            read n1
        call add
          read x2
          call multiply
            call multiply
              code 2
              call lorentz-form
                read x0
                read x1
                read x2
                read n0
                read n1
                read n2
            read n2
        call add
          read y0
          call multiply
            call multiply
              code 2
              call lorentz-form
                read y0
                read y1
                read y2
                read n0
                read n1
                read n2
            read n0
        call add
          read y1
          call multiply
            call multiply
              code 2
              call lorentz-form
                read y0
                read y1
                read y2
                read n0
                read n1
                read n2
            read n1
        call add
          read y2
          call multiply
            call multiply
              code 2
              call lorentz-form
                read y0
                read y1
                read y2
                read n0
                read n1
                read n2
            read n2
      call lorentz-form
        read x0
        read x1
        read x2
        read y0
        read y1
        read y2

rule a-reflection-is-an-involution-t
  seat x0, like integer
  seat x1, like integer
  seat x2, like integer
  seat n0, like integer
  seat n1, like integer
  seat n2, like integer
  have line
